  "full_response": "# Troubleshooting Guide: Unintentionally Committed Sensitive Information\n\n## Immediate Steps\n1. **Perform a Full Code Review**: Check the entire repository, including branch histories, pull requests, project wikis, API settings, etc., any other sensitive information.\n\n2. **Generate New Credentials**:\n   - **AWS Credentials**: Go to the [AWS IAM Console](https://.aws.amazon.com/iam/), create new IAM policies (avoid using root credentials), and new users.\n   - **GitHub Token**: Visit the [GitHub documentationhttps://docs.github.com/en/authentication/keeping-your-account-and-data-secure/creating-a-personal-access-token) to generate a new token.\n   - **Database Password: Follow your database's credential management practices (managed with IAM roles, encrypted with a password manager, etc.) to generate a new password.\n\n3. **Remove Sensitive Information**: Run your version control system to search and remove/replace the committed secrets (Bash, PowerShell, Git commands due to regex).\n\n4. **Rebase if Necessary**: If you've pushed to remote before removing the sensitive data, use the rebase operation to remove the secret from history or create a new commit after it.\n\n5. **Force Push to Remote Repository** (Warning: This rewrites history!):\n\n   ```bash\n   git push HEAD --force\n   ```\n\n6. **Generate a New GPG Key/GitHub App Certificate** if the repo’s existing GPG key or GitHub app certificate was compromised.\n\n7. **Verify Repository Status**: Scrub any instance of sensitive data to make sure they are not inadvertently accessible anywhere. Check for any on-premise or cloud storage linked to the repository.\n\n8. **Enable Two-Factor Authentication (2FA)**: For all accounts that were compromised, set up 2FA immediately to ensure an extra layer of security.\n\n9. **Delete the Compromised Token**: Remove the token from any services, disabling sessions that may have been created using it.\n\n10. **Notify Affected Parties**: If the compromised credentials posed a risk to other parties, inform them as soon as possible.\n\n11. **Limit Access as a Privacy Measure**: Temporarily or permanently revoke user privileges if necessary, after an audit.\n\n12. **Conduct a Security Audit**: Look at current repositories, clones, and branches that could use the exposed credentials:\n\n    ```bash\n    git log --all --name-only\n    ```\n\n",
